{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Phase 1 exit — confirmed root-cause analysis (read-only; no repair)\n\n### Problem and exact size layer\n\nA fresh single-branch clone at tickets tip `2fd4a1a62c92f534825097153dd1767e97ec4b47` reproduces the report, but the phrase “branch is over 1 GB” conflates distinct layers:\n\n- checked-out ticket tree: 112,788 files, 827,550,463 logical bytes, 1,116,577,792 bytes allocated in 4-KiB blocks;\n- `.git`: 358,767,066 logical bytes;\n- whole clone: 1,186,317,529 logical / 1,475,424,256 allocated bytes;\n- reachable Git pack: 322.57 MiB including index (`git count-objects -vH`), not 1 GiB.\n\nThe immediate >1-GiB threshold is therefore the checked-out tree’s allocated footprint: 827.6 MB of real file content plus 289.0 MB of filesystem block slack.\n\n### Proven causal account\n\n1. **The earlier reclaim succeeded, but measured a different thing.** Rewrite baseline `1300f383c75b10231fbd814cb9f6a1dfa269a26e` preserved the HEAD tree byte-for-byte and bounded the pack/history. The old pre-rewrite tip `68a705df011fb7b5e5f97481312611ecb8d758cb` is absent; a fresh clone has only local/remote `tickets`, no tags, no unreachable objects, and no reflog pin. Since that baseline, the live tree grew 290,690,203 -> 827,550,463 logical bytes and 396,152,832 -> 1,116,577,792 allocated bytes. This is new reachable data, not resurrected history.\n\n2. **A resolved outbound reconciler defect generated the anomalous payload.** Commit `27b868ba55eb5f699d3c4b0b72891aaeb969eef3` (story `emersed-specific-mutt`, `789c-1251-fc21-4cf6`) introduced the persistent comment-ID map but assumed successful `add_comment` returned a top-level Jira comment `id`. Cloud/ACLI actually returns a batch envelope with no top-level comment ID. The original `_record_comment_id` silently returned, production’s map stayed empty, the primary identity skip never fired, and rich comments whose bodies changed through ADF/wiki round-tripping were posted again every hourly pass. This mechanism was previously proven at runtime on bug `irongrey-chubby-oxpecker`; the explicit `caused_by` link to `emersed-specific-mutt` is recorded here.\n\n3. **A second, pre-existing marker-order defect let only the over-length echoes re-enter the local store.** Decoration appended `RECONCILER_MARKER` after the body, then right-truncation removed it. Jira therefore held 836 markerless, exactly-limit bot echoes on REB-1567, REB-1931, and REB-2605. The inbound loop-breaker could not recognize them as reconciler echoes, so their distinct Jira IDs were validly imported as distinct local COMMENT events. That defect was separately proven/fixed under `unreal-empathetic-illadopsis` (`5931-78a3-95d2-44cd`); its origin is rigorously unattributable inside rebar because marker and truncation arrived together in pre-history import `b534f47a7f`.\n\n4. **Append-only compaction amplified the imported bodies.** Six large bodies appear in 127–128 approximately hourly copies from 2026-08-14 06:53Z through 2026-08-21 04:41Z, all authored `reconciler`, concentrated in:\n   - `undyed-high-kiwi`: 174,969,874 bytes;\n   - `icicled-unstable-urial`: 69,311,996 bytes;\n   - `weary-wavy-hen`: 28,633,659 bytes.\n\n   There are 760 redundant COMMENT files totaling 18,496,834 bytes. Compaction writes a new full snapshot and renames every folded source `.retired`; current policy retains retired events and snapshots permanently pending causal-stability GC. Consequently the same bodies occupy another 17,164,261 bytes in active snapshots and 212,058,189 bytes in retired snapshots. Conservative redundant footprint: **247,719,284 bytes**, a 13.39x amplification over the redundant source events, 29.93% of the live tree, 90.77% of those three directories, and 46.14% of logical growth since the rewrite baseline. Snapshot size versus comment count correlations are 0.997, 0.99999, and 0.981.\n\n5. **Other retained data and block slack explain the rest and the final threshold crossing.** Current logical tree categories are: retired snapshots 254,853,040; REVIEW_RESULT 238,486,830; other retired events 127,483,175; active snapshots 95,128,343; enrichment lifecycle 71,921,418; other 25,559,514; `.bridge_state` 14,118,143 bytes. Of 112,788 files, 89,577 are <=4 KiB: they contain 132,902,547 logical bytes but allocate 366,903,296, contributing 234,000,749 bytes of slack. After the duplicate loop stopped, 34,289 enrichment lifecycle files added 55,297,265 logical but 140,500,992 allocated bytes; that was 66.3% of post-Aug-20 allocation growth and pushed the checkout across 1 GiB.\n\n### Rival explanations tested\n\n- **Stale refs / unreachable pre-rewrite history:** rejected by the fresh clone’s ref set, absent old tip, and empty `git fsck --full --unreachable --no-reflogs`.\n- **Commit count as the direct gigabyte:** rejected. There are 151,184 commits and 18,977 merges, but packed commit entries are only 27,548,070 bytes; trees are 60,149,981 and blobs 235,538,455. Raw object-size sums (36.8 GB) compress/delta to a 323.2-MB pack and must not be treated as disk usage.\n- **Many small files alone:** rejected as a complete explanation. Their block slack is material (234.0 MB), but the tree also contains 827.6 MB of logical payload.\n- **Whole-file duplication:** rejected. Exact hashing found only 127 duplicate 21-byte tombstones; timestamps, Jira IDs, UUIDs, signatures, and nested snapshot metadata make the large files distinct even when bodies repeat.\n- **Renewed bridge-state churn as the checkout root:** rejected. `peer_confirmations.json` has a real volatile-field rewrite defect, but its current file is 1,400,452 bytes and its 417 unique historical blobs occupy about 6,077,416 packed bytes. It is separately filed, not misattributed to the >1-GiB tree.\n\n### Resolution state\n\nFix `8d98c298fa816acf51b1bae069f36695066b2695` records a successful post even when ACLI cannot return a comment ID. It is an ancestor of workflow head `8fa8f36b6482b2371128607a63c5483a9783e887`. Run 32447039101 posted the then-unmapped backlog; run 32447697242 imported the final six repeated events; run 32447928090 reported zero inbound comment differences and converged. No later copy of the six anomalous bodies exists through the frozen tickets tip. Marker-preserving fix `6724e79e8ff857b54dd36a62006a89445fc10ad6` is also on current main.\n\n**Conclusion:** the reconciler loop really did create a major anomalous payload, but it is no longer recurring. The current >1-GiB checkout is retained historical damage amplified by full snapshots/permanent `.retired` retention, combined with normal-but-unbounded review/enrichment payloads and small-file allocation. The prior pack-history reclaim did not regress; its pack-only acceptance metric could not detect this tree/allocation growth.\n\n### Residual dispositions\n\n- `irongray-unlucky-discus`: safe targeted reclaim for the 247,719,284-byte duplicate footprint.\n- `epidemic-chocolaty-imago`: enforce separate pack, logical-tree, allocated-tree, file/slack, and whole-clone budgets.\n- `degradable-appalling-cavy`: bound/reclaim 238,486,830 bytes of active+retired REVIEW_RESULT sidecars.\n- `proposed-kitschy-firefly`: stop volatile `confirmed_pass` / `confirmed_at` refreshes from rewriting the full peer-confirmation sidecar.\n\nNo production files were changed and no repair/rewrite was attempted in this investigation."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787524039364476001,"uuid":"e636eaba-39a5-4450-ab0e-7f672a9bbdae"}